    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d614">

                <pb facs="0821" n="745" sameAs="frus:frus1935v02/pg745"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.5431/126: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Switzerland</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Wilson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Bern</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-01-31T10:00:00+01:00">January 31, 1935—10
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 31—6:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>7. Stucki<anchor n="2" xml:id="d614-f1"/> yesterday implied that serious obstacle
                    to reaching a trade agreement with the United States would be the uncertainty of
                    future value of the dollar. He argued that even though the United States granted
                    tariff concessions such benefits would be modified or canceled by further
                    currency devaluation.</p>
                <p>The Department has presumably met this argument before and I should be happy to
                    learn how it has been answered.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d614-f1" n="2" xml:id="d614-f1-content">
                <p>Walter Stucki, Chief of the Commercial Division of the Swiss Federal Department
                    of Public Economy.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
